  "headline": "The Corrosion Engineer serves as the front-line defender against the insidious electrochemical and microbiological destruction of buried cross-country energy pipelines and terminal process assets. While enterprise asset management software and standalone cathodic protection (CP) calculators serve as data repositories, they leave the engineer buried under tens of thousands of un-reconciled field survey records, spreadsheet coupon logs, and disconnected electromagnetic interference reports. On a single 500-kilometer cross-country transmission corridor, evaluating annual test-station potentials, continuous Close Interval Potential Surveys (CIPS), and Direct Current Voltage Gradient (DCVG) coating defect surveys requires manually plotting millions of millivolt readings and cross-referencing high-voltage AC power transmission co-locations. Because survey data arrives from third-party field contractors in fragmented spreadsheets with uncorrected IR-drop errors, engineers spend weeks manually aligning potential profiles to find under-protected segments. Missing an under-protected anomaly or mistaking a disbonded tape shield for adequate polarization can allow external stress corrosion cracking or microbiological pitting to breach pipe wall thickness in months, triggering catastrophic pipeline ruptures and multi-million-dollar emergency shutdowns. Deploying a dedicated 7-agent hyper-specialized corrosion engineering squad saves 105.25 hours per major asset corrosion assessment and remediation campaign, eliminating manual data reconciliation drag, automating AC/DC stray current mitigation sizing, optimizing chemical inhibition dosing, and accelerating statutory compliance filings.",
